  <div style="display: flex; gap: 40px;">
    <div style="flex: 1;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 24pt; margin-bottom: 20px;">On My Plate</h2>
      <p style="font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 20px;">By <span data-field="author_name_2">{{ author_name_2 }}</span>, RMFU President</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">Uncertainty reigns in the agricultural industry. In my last column here I talked about that uncertainty, especially as it relates to trade wars and federal agriculture policy.</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">Unfortunately, the trade wars have escalated and farmers and ranchers are suffering as a result of the trade war with China and the lack of congressional action on the proposed trade agreement with Canada and Mexico.</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">Although the federal government is providing some assistance to farmers and ranchers, the so-called "bail-out" is not the long-term answer. What farmers and ranchers want is access to the global markets and fair trade policies.</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">In September a delegation of Rocky Mountain Farmers Union members will join hundreds of other like-minded producers to participate in the National Farmers Union annual legislative fly-in to Washington, D.C. Working together in teams, we stop by every congressional office.</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">We've been doing this for decades to put a face on American agriculture and tell our stories of how federal policies are impacting our operations. We also share boots on the ground comments to lawmakers who may not be as understanding of challenges facing agriculture and rural communities.</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">Farmers Union has referred to itself as "The Voice of the Family Farmers." The key word here is "Family." We work together for the common good.</p>
      <div style="border-left: 4px solid #ccc; padding-left: 15px; margin: 20px 0;">
        <p style="font-style: italic;">"We work together for the common good."</p>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">Whether you are a member live on the Western Slope, Eastern Plains, or in the San Luis Valley, the one thing you have in common as a member of Farmers Union is family.</p>
    </div>

    <div style="flex: 1;">
      <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">might have an open range cattle operation near Santa Fe or Cheyenne, or live along a river valley in northern Wyoming or eastern New Mexico. We have common roots in the soil. We are all family. If you have an organic farm-to-market farmers operation in Denver or are raising sugar beets near Wellington, you know that Farmers Union speaks for families. We do not always agree, we may share different points of view. Yet we work together for the common good. In today's argumentative environment, this makes us uncommon.</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">Congress has 435 representatives and 100 senators from 50 states. These states can be thousands of miles apart and represent distinctly different historical, economic, cultural, and geographical values. In order to pass a Food and Farm Bill, an annual budget, or ratify a trade agreement requires some level of agreement and a measure of negotiation for the common good. The more diverse the group is, the more difficult it is to dig in his heels and insisting things is "my way or the highway." The more we all agree, the more we can accomplish. Farmers America can't afford this. Farmers and ranchers face enough uncertainty. Getting caught up in partisan issues usually results in getting caught up in the process.</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">And this is why Farmers Union's policy is written by you and other members who show up, stand up, and share your stories. We find common ground by getting together at our chapter conventions, and state and national conventions, to listen and learn from each other. When we go to our state legislatures or Capitol Hill, we are speaking with one voice, a voice that represents the families who make a living farming and ranching from the ground up. Our job is to put you at the table and give you a voice in the discussion.</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">We are family at Farmers Union, whether we are in our own homes or within the organization. Rather than compete with each other, we cooperate. Rather than criticize each other, we collaborate. We celebrate when every individual and every community succeeds. Isn't this what family is for?</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">The value of family in farming and ranching is on my mind. Earlier this year, my wife Judy passed away during surgery. Together, we raised a family, we built a farm one field at a time, and we built a life together. I did not always agree with her. We always listened respectfully to each other's point of view, and we compromised. To compromise means giving in and not getting what you want. But that's how we build a life and move forward. All of my accomplishments during my career in farming and in ag education are thanks to the team Judy and I created. When it comes to family, count your blessings.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

