      <h3 style="font-size: 18pt; margin-bottom: 10px;">Hemp May Not Be Colorado's Next Gold Rush, Yet Farmers See Black Ink Coming From Green Fields</h3>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">Brian Mitchell inspects one of several hemp test plots he's been planting at the Colorado State University Research, Development and Education Center northeast of Fort Collins.</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">By Bob Kjelland, Union Farmer Editor</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">Here is everything you need to know about hemp in one paragraph. One, it is not the miracle crop that is going to save agriculture. Two, lots of individuals and companies believe there is money to be made selling hemp products, yet finding a reliable market is proving to be a challenge. Three, it is a value-adding cottage industry into another commodity crop like corn.</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">Actually, there is a lot more to know about hemp and those who are working to establish a new ag industry from crop to consumer. The state of Colorado is a leader in the industry, and the state is making many offers to those who are willing to learn everything you can before you plant a seed. The state is also making many offers to those who are willing to learn everything you can before you plant a seed.</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">Farmers (and lawmakers) in Colorado, New Mexico, and Wyoming are not remote from the conversation. They want to know if hemp is a viable alternative to other crops. The answer is, it depends on a lot of variables from soil and climate conditions to access to markets. The state is working to develop regulations for growing hemp and even being the first</p>
