      <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">Brian Mitchell inspects one of several hemp test plots he's been planting at the Colorado State University Research, Development and Education Center northeast of Fort Collins.</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">State to create a specific director of hemp in its Department of Agriculture. Colorado was ahead of the curve partly because of the state's willingness to experiment with a new crop. The state's regulatory framework is still in place and harvested hemp acres this year.</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">Worldwide, China is the top producer of hemp, followed by Canada, the United States, and France.</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">Hemp, Marijuana and THC</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">In America, hemp - once a staple crop for farmers - fell out of favor because of tetrahyldrocannabinol, or THC, the chemical that accounts for the psychoactive "high" derived from marijuana. Hemp and marijuana plants look similar, smell similar, and are easily confused for each other. In the eyes of law enforcement, hemp looks like marijuana. In general, hemp</p>
