      <h2 style="font-size: 24pt; margin-bottom: 20px;">In The Field</h2>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">leaves are skinny and shiny, with the plant itself appearing tall and skinny. Marijuana is bushy and shorter. Hemp that is being grown for fiber production tends to be taller, with the leaves gathered toward the top. Hemp grown specifically for CBD oil is shorter and bushier. The challenge for law enforcement?</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">In chemical terms, marijuana might have up to 30 percent THC, while hemp has less than 0.3 percent THC. The difference depends on labwork rather than a visual inspection.</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">Colorado passed Amendment 64 in 2012 that effectively legalized marijuana for recreational use and the measure cleared the way for industrial hemp. The state's Department of Agriculture made space for a new industrial hemp regulatory program that included specific state responsibilities.</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">"We have state calls asking us for advice. We are ahead of the curve," says Colorado Commissioner of Agriculture Kate Greenberg. The department is holding stakeholder hearings to evaluate the entire industry under the Colorado Hemp Advancement & Management Plan (CHAMP). "We are getting input from all the stakeholders, from farmers to processors, and research and development of seed, cultivation, transportation, and processing. We are working to create a regulatory framework that will set up nationwide in pushing leading edge." The CHAMP final report is due for release next spring.</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">Brian Mitchell, a PhD student at Colorado State University Research, Development and Education Center, is participating in the CHAMP initiative. His says Colorado is well along on the learning curve when it comes to hemp. And, he adds, we still have a lot to learn. Mitchell is weeks away from presenting his research findings on hemp as measured</p>
    </div>

    <div style="flex: 1;">
      <h3 style="font-size: 18pt; margin-bottom: 10px;">Hemp Is In The Roots Of U.S. Agriculture</h3>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">There's nothing new about hemp, except that there is a 50-year history of hemp in the United States. In the U.S. past, (predominantly in the George Washington and Tennessee Valley) hemp was a staple crop. It was used to maintain fishing nets and create ropes and sail canvas. Jefferson used hemp to make clothing.</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">The 1937 Marijuana Tax Act legally considered hemp to be marijuana, which led to the banning of hemp cultivation. The ban was excluded from the Controlled Substances Act in the 2018 Farm Bill.</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">Yet in 1942, USDA produced the film "Hemp For Victory" to encourage farmers to plant the crop to offset supply shortages during World War II. The U.S. Navy needed rope so the hemp was needed. They did, and then hemp went back to being viewed as an undesirable plant for its link to marijuana.</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">At the federal level, marijuana remains an illegal drug. In Colorado and a number of other states, marijuana is legal for medicinal and recreational uses. In Nebraska, marijuana is</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">been no need for such processing facilities. Without processing facilities, farmers are reluctant to invest too much in a corp without knowing when local markets will be up and running and reliable.</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">It is not for lack of innovation or entrepreneurship. Hemp fiber can be used to make ropes and shirts and paper products including many products currently made from wood pulp. Hemp fiber can also be used to make building materials, and other techniques to create durable goods.</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">Again, this is nothing new. In 1941, Henry Ford gave serious attention to building cars and interior items from "cellulose-plastics" made from hemp and soybeans. Ford's company was a pioneer in the use of hemp for industrial purposes.</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">So, is hemp going to be a great feedstock crop for ethanol production? Don't count on it. There are other crops that are much better at being converted to fuel based on their tried-and-true properties and cost structure, explains Mitchell.</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">"The market for raw materials associated with hemp stalks is growing. Research around hemp uses for fiber, or hemp-based"
