        <p>Clear, mountain streams as essential to the brewing process. If you see a "Colorado Native" beer made by AC Golden Brewing Company, you can be sure that the hops are from the Miller Coors plant in Golden. Even the big brewers are using local ingredients, and the trend is growing.</p>
        <p>So, streams of water and golden seas of barley are close at hand for the major brewers. Hops, however, are brought in from the Pacific Northwest simply because that's where they grow well based on soil, climate, and due to generations of farmers who know the crop.</p>
        <p>From the 1980s on, the notion of local beer took on a flavor of its own as small microbreweries began popping up from Denver north to Fort Collins. Today, some of those earliest breweries have grown into large, successful operations that are now part of the state's agricultural economy.</p>
